Japanese sample EPUBs for exercising the reader by hand on a device. Kept apart
from test/epubs so this fork's own material, with its own provenance, does not
mix into the upstream set. Nothing here is loaded by the gtest suites; copy a
file to an SD card and open it.

Two kinds of material live here: four builds of one novel (below), and one
merged diagnostic suite (vertical-test-suite.epub, last section).

The four files
--------------

One book, built four ways:

                         full CSS (~144 KB)      minimal CSS (~2 KB)
  vertical, ppd=rtl      test_vertical_ja        test_vertical_ja_minicss
  horizontal, ppd=ltr    test_horizontal_ja      test_horizontal_ja_minicss

The chapter XHTML is byte-identical across a row, and across a column it differs
by one attribute -- class="vrtl" against class="hltr" on the body element. So the
set isolates two variables independently:

  along a row     writing mode, with the stylesheet held constant
  down a column   stylesheet size, with the writing mode held constant

That makes a difference attributable. A rendering fault that appears in the
minimal-CSS file too is in the layout engine; one that only appears with the full
stylesheet is in CSS handling. A fault present in both writing modes is not a
tategaki bug, whatever it looks like.

The horizontal pair also serves as the regression check for vertical work: the
vertical branch must not disturb it, and having the same text on both sides means
a regression shows up as a difference rather than as a hunch.

What the text covers
--------------------

12 chapters, 196 ruby instances, 41 of them read in Latin script. They include
annotations long enough to overhang their base word and to overhang the head and
foot of a column. Also list items, tilde variants (U+FF5E, U+301C, U+223C,
U+02DC), fullwidth and halfwidth digit runs of one, two and three digits,
fullwidth operators (＝ ＜ ＞ ≠ ≒), exclamation/question pairs, Greek letters,
and line-break positions where kinsoku rules apply. Most of the vertical fixes on
this branch were found with it.

The last chapter is about ruby alone, and each of its paragraphs is one question:
long marks and brackets inside a reading, readings that are a single Latin letter
or carry a hyphen, period, apostrophe or space, readings that are only digits
(where the body's own rule sets one and two upright and turns three), readings in
Cyrillic and Greek, readings that mix scripts, one-character bases carrying
readings far longer than themselves and set next to each other so they have to
collide, monoruby that fits its base exactly against monoruby that does not, and
a long passage seeded with annotated words so that some of them land on a column
break -- which they do not yet survive.

Provenance
----------

Text by OSAKANA TARO, published on Kakuyomu under the handle memoro and reused
here by its author. CSS derived from the EBPAJ EPUB 3 production guide template
set, ver. 1.1.4.

The EPUBs are built from that Kakuyomu source by NovelToEpub, a PowerShell script
at https://github.com/osakanataro/NovelToEpub, so they can be rebuilt rather than
hand-edited -- fix the text on Kakuyomu, re-run, replace the files.

Run the four from one working directory of your own, then copy the results here.
KAKUYOMU below stands for https://kakuyomu.jp/works/2912051602969738484.

  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -MaxCacheAgeHours 0.1 -OutputDir test_vertical_ja.epub
  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -MinimalCss -OutputDir test_vertical_ja_minicss.epub
  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -Horizontal -OutputDir test_horizontal_ja.epub
  pwsh NovelToEpub.txt -Url KAKUYOMU -Horizontal -MinimalCss -OutputDir test_horizontal_ja_minicss.epub

-OutputDir names the file when its value ends in .epub, and a directory otherwise.
Naming each one is what keeps the four apart: without it every run writes the
work's own title and the previous file is gone.

Run them from the same directory, and not from this one. The episode cache is a
.cache directory beside the output, so a shared working directory is what lets the
four share one fetch -- and pointing them here would leave that cache in the repo,
which .gitignore does not cover outside the root.

The script caches each episode it fetches, and -MaxCacheAgeHours defaults to 0,
which disables age-based expiry: a cached episode is reused however old it is.
Pass -MaxCacheAgeHours 0.1 on the *first* command only, as above, so it re-checks
Kakuyomu for revisions. The remaining three run within those six minutes and
reuse what the first fetched, so the site is polled once rather than four times.

Rebuild all four together. They are only worth what their shared text is worth,
and the source gains chapters over time -- regenerating one alone silently breaks
the comparison the set exists for.

vertical-test-suite.epub
------------------------

Diagnostic tests merged into one book so they survive as a reusable set. Each
test is preceded by a guide page in Japanese saying what to look at and what
counts as a pass, so the book can be judged on the device without consulting
anything else. Thirty chapters, in guide/body pairs:

  1  OPF namespace prefixes. The suite's own OPF carries an opf: prefix on
     every element (package/metadata/manifest/spine/item/itemref), so this
     book opening at all -- title 「縦書き表示検証テスト集」, author, and eight
     TOC entries intact -- is itself the pass condition (upstream #3001,
     prefix-independent element matching).
  2  Paragraph alignment against auto indent. Classless paragraphs under
     justify/left/center/right alignment, next to a text-indent:3em reference
     that bypasses the auto CJK first-line indent and its alignment gate.
  3  Halfwidth punctuation in vertical lines. Halfwidth kagi brackets
     (U+FF62/63), prolonged sound mark (U+FF70) and middle dot (U+FF65)
     against their fullwidth counterparts in the same column; the halfwidth
     forms must rotate and center like the fullwidth ones.
  4  Ideographic-space indentation. A leading U+3000 must survive as a
     one-character indent; two otherwise identical paragraphs make its loss
     visible as stars starting at the same height.
  5  Entity references. The body file carries the real-world XHTML 1.1
     external-DTD DOCTYPE, under which expat routes undefined entities to
     the reader's own lookup table instead of failing; the text mixes the
     five XML-predefined entities (expat resolves those itself, &apos;
     included) with table-resolved ones (&hellip; &mdash; &copy; &deg;).
     Note the #3122 fix proper (adding &apos; to that table) is NOT
     reachable from chapter display -- expat expands &apos; before the
     table is consulted; the table entry matters on the non-expat paths
     (OPDS description flattening, KOReader sync matching). This chapter
     verifies the adjacent table path. A plain <!DOCTYPE html> file with
     &hellip; is a fatal "undefined entity" parse error by design -- the
     first version of this test did exactly that and bricked the chapter.
  6  Trailing junk after </html> (upstream #3134). The body file carries
     deliberately non-XML garbage after its closing tag; the chapter
     rendering at all is the pass condition. Unfixed parsers error out and
     show nothing. This file is intentionally not well-formed XML, so
     strict validators (epubcheck) will flag it -- that is the test.
  7  Letterlike Symbols and variation selectors (2026-08-23, from a scan of
     513 real Kakuyomu/Narou EPUBs against the generated fonts). ℃ ℓ № Å ℘
     sat in a preset gap between "punctuation" and "symbols" -- neither
     starts before U+2150, so the whole Letterlike Symbols block was absent
     even though the source fonts carry it; ℃ alone appeared in 57 of the
     513 books. ⭐ ⬛ ⬇ ⬜ ⬅ ⬆ ⌒ ⌘ are the other real corpus hits, added as
     narrow ranges rather than their full parent blocks. The body also
     carries literal U+FE0E/U+FE0F (VS15/VS16) right after ☆ ♪ ♥ -- these
     are zero-width presentation-style selectors with no glyph in any font,
     but before the parser fix they fell through to the replacement glyph
     and drew a visible tofu box; the corpus scan found 1,288 of them across
     38 books (up to 275 in one). The VS16 base is ♥ U+2665 (heart SUIT, in
     JIS and every source font), deliberately not ❤ U+2764 (Dingbats heavy
     heart): no source font carries 2764, so a sentence built on it shows a
     replacement glyph regardless of the selector fix -- the first version
     of this chapter did exactly that and the on-device photo was misread
     as a pass. Pass condition: the symbols in test (1) and (2) render as
     their real glyphs rather than tofu, and the ☆/♪/♥ sentences in test
     (3) read with no gap or box after the mark.
  8  Supplementary-plane kanji (2026-08-23). 𠮟 U+20B9F -- the JIS X 0213 form
     of しかる, found in 6 of the 513 books -- sits above the Basic
     Multilingual Plane (4-byte UTF-8) and needed two separate fixes: the
     font generator's presets stopped at U+FFEF, so the glyph was never a
     rasterization candidate, and isUprightInVertical() (VerticalTextUtils.h)
     didn't know the supplementary range either, so even a font that carried
     it would draw it rotated -- the sideways path meant for Latin runs.
     Pass condition: 𠮟 renders as the real kanji (not tofu) AND stands
     upright in the column like its neighbours, not rotated like the Latin
     "NG" two lines below it.
  9  CJK Compatibility Ideographs and Forms (2026-08-23, from a 955-book scan
     of commercial EPUBs -- text itself wasn't read or kept, only aggregate
     codepoint stats and titles). 溺 U+F9EC (10 books), 蓮 U+F999 (5), 煉
     U+F993 (3) are Compatibility-Ideograph duplicates of kanji the JIS
     X 0213 allowlist already carries in their unified BMP form; that same
     allowlist gates F900-FAFF, so a font can carry the glyph and the book
     still draw tofu unless the exact duplicate codepoint is on the list.
     ⦿ U+29BF (1010 uses in one book, as a bullet marker) and the vertical
     presentation forms ︱/︲ U+FE31-32 (em dash) and ︽/︾ U+FE3D-3E (《》)
     were simply absent from every interval preset. The last two also
     needed isUprightInVertical() and kinsoku entries -- they're glyphs
     pre-shaped for vertical text, so rotating them (the treatment their
     horizontal originals — and 《》 get) would turn an already-vertical
     stroke sideways. Pass condition: (1) 溺 蓮 煉 render as real kanji;
     (2) ⦿ renders as a filled circle, not tofu; (3) ︱︱ reads as a
     straight vertical stroke, not rotated; (4) ︽注意︾ stands upright,
     brackets right-side-out, not flipped like 《》 would be if rotated.
     BIZ UD's source font lacks 溺/煉/︱/︽/︾ outright (only 蓮 and ⦿);
     that face draws whatever its existing missing-glyph fallback does for
     those five, same as any other rare glyph it was already missing.

 11  Ideographic-space indent against the reader's own, and vertical
     paragraph alignment. Paperback EPUBs write the first-line indent as a
     literal U+3000; if the reader then adds its own, the line starts two
     characters in. The same chapter checks that choosing centre/right
     alignment does not silently switch the indent off -- vertical columns
     are laid right to left regardless, so alignment changing only the
     indent reads as the indent vanishing on its own. From two fixes in the
     Yomuka fork (#21, #20) and a report against Matcha (#163).
 12  Inter-word spaces in vertical Latin runs. Horizontal layout adds word
     spacing at render time; vertical layout stores one advance per cell, so
     a dropped space turns `Do you see the boy` into one run. Runs of spaces
     and newlines must collapse to one, and leading/trailing ones must go.
     From the Yomuka fork (#11).
 13  Small kana placement in vertical text. The horizontal glyph for ぁぃゃゅょっ
     sits low-left inside its em box; used as-is vertically the character
     appears to sink. The rule is JLREQ 2.1.2 note 1: vertically the face
     goes "天地中央で右寄り" -- centred top-to-bottom, shifted right. Not
     high-right: the JP fork's report cites 3.1.4 (a different section, on
     runs of consecutive punctuation) and its fix moves the glyph up as well
     as right, which overshoots the spec. The pass condition here follows
     the spec, and a glyph riding high is recorded as its own deviation. The
     advance must not change, so column breaks, line spacing and ruby stay
     put. From the JP fork (#98), citation corrected against JLREQ.
 14  Long-paragraph and mid-flush stress. An Aozora-shaped body -- no inner
     paragraph tags, <br/> separators, several thousand CJK characters in
     one block -- which is where three failures live: the word vector's
     doubling reallocation aborting on a fragmented heap (JP fork #74), a
     mid-flush emitting a visibly short final column (JP fork #53), and the
     1024-codepoint advance table overflowing so later glyphs get a zero
     advance and overlap (Yomuka fork #10). Ruby is sprinkled through the
     long block so a flush boundary can land inside a group. The 1300
     distinct kanji in the second half were each verified to have real glyph
     extents in all three shipped SD fonts by reading the .cpfont tables --
     a character the font lacks would draw as tofu, which is exactly what
     the overlap being looked for also resembles.

     Its guide also carries the pass conditions for ruby that overhangs its
     base (JLREQ 3.3.8), since this chapter is where ruby meets column
     breaks: the overhang may ride over kana, the prolonged sound mark,
     small kana and an ideographic space by up to the ruby's own size, and
     an implementation that refuses to overhang at all is equally
     acceptable -- what fails either way is ruby colliding with the
     neighbouring column. This is the open "ruby distribution across a
     column break" item finally given a written pass condition.

 15  Consecutive punctuation, and brackets at the line head and line end.
     Closing brackets, commas and full stops are half-width glyphs that take
     a half-em of space beside kanji or kana to fill one em cell; when they
     run together that space is dropped in specific places so the line does
     not gape (JLREQ 2.5.2 and 3.1.4: 。」 and 」、 set solid with the half-em
     after, 、「 and 」「 take the half-em between). The same chapter watches
     the line-head/line-end rules -- no closing bracket, comma or full stop
     alone at the head of a column, no opening bracket alone at the foot --
     and asks only that the line-head opening bracket be treated the same
     way every time (JLREQ 3.1.5 permits more than one convention, not a mix
     of them). Dialogue-heavy fiction hits this on every page. Written from
     JLREQ directly rather than carried over from another fork.

Tests 11-14 came from closed PRs and issues in the sibling forks rather than
from a fault seen here: they are cases those projects hit on real books, so
this tree's behaviour under them is simply unknown until the book is run.

Every chapter title carries its test number ("テストN 解説/本文: ..."), so the
status bar alone says which test is on screen. The unit is the test, not the
chapter: each test is a guide and a body, so numbering by chapter would put
the same number on two consecutive rows of the chapter picker and hide the
fact that they are a pair. (The image book keeps 第N章 -- its nine chapters
are each their own thing, so nothing repeats there.)

Test 14's body numbers its own lines, 〔1〕..〔40〕 through the long paragraph
and 〈1〉..〈22〉 through the kanji run, and gives each line its own ruby word.
The first cut repeated one sentence forty times: it still proved the chapter
does not abort, but a short column or a misplaced ruby could not be located
in the text, and every ruby sat at the same offset so whether one ever met a
column break was left to chance.

The spine is page-progression-direction=rtl and every chapter is class="vrtl",
so the whole book takes the vertical path. The stylesheet is a few hundred
bytes and minimal on purpose: the base p rule sets no text-align (test 2
depends on that), and the chapters that need justified text carry a p-justify
class per paragraph instead.

Unlike the four novels above, this one is not generated from anywhere: the
markup is written by hand and zipped by a throwaway Python script. To change
it, unzip, edit, and rezip -- with the mimetype entry first and stored
uncompressed, as EPUB requires.

vertical-image-test.epub
------------------------

Images mixed into vertical text, kept as its own book so an image-path crash
cannot take the main suite down with it. Another fork (ponto1216-ai) shipped a
mitigation for paragraph-final inline images sinking to mid-screen in vertical
mode; this book exists to see whether we inherit that class of bug before any
image work starts. Guide page plus five chapters (v5 adds a second guide and two
more chapters, below; every chapter title carries its number): a 48x48 inline image
mid-sentence and at paragraph end, the block-illustration chapter with the same
300x200 stripes referenced four ways (../ grayscale PNG, same-directory PNG,
JPEG, RGB-type PNG -- an isolation ladder from when images failed and the
suspect list was long), a near-full-page 560x760 image with text continuing
after it, gaiji-style images sized to one character cell via CSS (img.gaiji at
1em; check under both a gothic and a mincho font, since the sibling fork's
detection once broke on glyph-width assumptions), and 1-bit / 4-bit PNGs. The
PNGs are generated by a few lines of Python -- an X, stripes, a checkerboard,
a gradient -- so they are recognizable at a glance.

What the 2026-08-21 investigation with this book established (details in the
repo-external dev log): image display on the X3 is gated by two contiguous-heap
walls, not by paths or formats. Reading a deflated zip entry wants a 32KB
inflate window, and PNGdec's decode struct wants ~60KB in one piece -- neither
is available mid-build or mid-render once Japanese fonts and the vertical arena
are resident. Build now places the images; render falls back to empty
placeholder boxes, drawn at horizontal-model coordinates that overlap vertical
text. The diag firmware writes the whole trail to /image-diag.txt per image.
The known fix direction is build-time pixel-cache pregeneration (the
ponto1216-ai fork shipped exactly that); parked for now.

v5 (2026-08-28) rebuilds the second half of the book around a survey of how 955
commercial vertical EPUBs actually write their illustrations -- markup shape and
sizing only, no text read. Three findings, and each one contradicted something
this book had assumed:

  Sizing comes from CSS classes, never from width/height attributes or inline
  style, and a picture is fitted with max-width/max-height rather than a fixed
  width. Chapter 8 is that shape: max-100%, max-80%, a wide picture, three gaiji
  variants, and one unclassed image as the control.

  Over half the illustrations are not img at all but an image element wrapped in
  svg -- 527 of the 955 books, about 70,000 sites, all writing it the same way
  (svg at 100%/100% with a viewBox, image inside). Chapter 9 is that form, and
  ends with the same picture as a plain img so a failure there separates "svg
  handling" from "images at all".

  Every image in every book was deflated -- not one STORED among more than
  100,000. So v4's decision to store this book's images uncompressed, taken
  because real EPUBs were believed to do that, was testing a case that does not
  occur. v5 puts them back to deflated. The 32KB inflate window is not an
  obstacle a book can be authored around; it has to be met in firmware.
